==Question #21==
{{Cquote2|I’d love to see a discussion between J-mods of what they think is the best way to get new players into OSRS. How do the team feel we can best boost membership and get newer players engaged with the game?}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_2kR-Ipwi4k&t=3455s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Light:''' I actually talked to Mod Squid who's working on something called a "content recommender", which I thought was like perfect to answer this question. I'll sum up really briefly. The 'content recommender' suggests quests and milestones to work towards based on your current progress in the game. The aim is basically to improve discoverability of content, help players setting their own goals by suggesting content, explaining that content - how and why it was chosen so that you can start to make decisions on your own about what content you should be doing in-game. It's not intended to be too hand-holdy. It's just meant to flag relevant content to you and teach players in that way.
* '''Mod Light:''' Mod Squid also gave me some thoughts on other areas of the game that would benefit, so apparently he feels the game is quite solo oriented. It's even more difficult to play with your friends if they're at a high level. I know this for a fact because all of the J-mods in the team are absolute gamers and I'm totally not so it's really difficult to catch up with them!
* '''Mod Light:''' It's definitely something that Mod Squid is wanting to look into. Gameplay-wise, he thinks we would benefit from fleshing out methods of training skills at lower levels. Tutorial-wise, he also wants to revisit that - even though the current tutorial is so iconic, the team mostly agrees that it doesn't do a great job of setting players up for success.
* '''Mod Elena:''' It's a super interesting topic and if I knew the perfect answer, we would probably have done it by now. There's no one good solution but there are plenty of different things we can do. I think improving the fresh player experience is really key because it doesn't matter how many people you get to try your game, if they quit after day one it didn't help you. We need to get people into the game and we need to keep them in the game. I think what Mod Squid is talking about could really help keep them in the game, which is really good in terms of introducing them - just making people download the Old School app or try the game at all.
* '''Mod Elena:''' I think it often comes down to the hype around new releases and people just telling their friends to come play with them. That makes me lean towards group content, because if you could tell your friend "Hey, this new thing launched in Old School RuneScape, a game that I play; do you want to come play with me?" then that's a good way to get people into the game. So maybe more group-based stuff early on in the game would be my ideal solution.
* '''Mod Tide:''' I think this question is pretty pretty broad because different things will get different people into the game. Some people might find that they want small incentives to be added when they do things during the game - for instance, they get 10 attack, they can get a small item upgrade or something like that. Other people might want more of an explanation on how to do stuff, or video tutorials for instance - so maybe making them available in game would be something to help get people into it.
* '''Mod Light:''' I know a lot of content creators do an amazing job of helping newer players get engaged and interested in content!
* '''Mod Kirby:''' I think the main problem is the tutorial just doesn't set the expectations of the game.  When we think of RuneScape we think of epic PvM encounters. or intense skilling, or quests - we have so many good quests! Our tutorial teaches you how to catch a shrimp, then cook some bread and it's like... these activities are not related! You don't really get that experience and I feel like that's probably like a more important selling point we can give to the players a lot earlier on.
* '''Mod Kirby:''' We're just missing an opportunity by not at least trying to give it to them. How do you write a tutorial that could cover like so much? The game has so much to offer, how do you like compartmentalize that in a 5-10 minute tutorial you can give the players and go "okay, here is our game".
* '''Mod Tide:''' One thing I'm thinking of that might be another good idea - what about a mentoring system? So if you're a new player, and you've said you're a new player, when you get off Tutorial Island you're put into a clan where there's all these different people. They have like a badge next to them to say "hey, these are mentors" and you could talk to them and ask them question, they could show you around the game help you out.